  "order": 10,
  "title": "The sharp keys in order",
  "summary": "Sharps are always added in the same order, so a number of sharps names one key.",
  "minutes": 6,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Order of sharps",
      "body": "Sharps are always added in the same order: F♯ C♯ G♯ D♯ A♯ E♯ B♯. G major has the first one, D major the first two, A major the first three, and so on. This is the **order of sharps**.\n\n**Why it matters:** If a key has three sharps, you know which three: F♯, C♯ and G♯. No guessing.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        66,
        61,
        68,
        63,
        70
      ],
      "labels": {
        "61": "2nd: C♯",
        "63": "4th: D♯",
        "66": "1st: F♯",
        "68": "3rd: G♯",
        "70": "5th: A♯"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "From the sharps to the key",
      "body": "Go up a half step from the last sharp and you reach the tonic. Three sharps end on G♯, a half step below A, so three sharps means A major.\n\n**Why it matters:** with this trick you can read any sharp key signature in a second.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        68,
        69
      ],
      "labels": {
        "68": "last sharp: G♯",
        "69": "A major"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "The sharp keys",
      "body": "G (1♯), D (2♯), A (3♯), E (4♯) and B (5♯).",
